In Java, both static and non-static (also known as instance) methods are used to perform operations on data. The key difference lies in how they are associated with the class and its instances.

A static method belongs to the class rather than any specific instance of the class. You can call a static method without creating an instance of the class. These methods can only access other static members of the class directly. They are useful for utility functions that don't rely on instance-specific data.

On the other hand, a non-static method is associated with an instance of the class. It requires an object of the class to be created before it can be called. Non-static methods can access both static and non-static members of the class because they operate on the instance context.

Here's a simple example:

```java
public class Example {
    int x = 0; // instance variable

    static void staticMethod() { // static method
        System.out.println("Static method called");
    }

    void nonStaticMethod() { // non-static method
        System.out.println("Non-static method called");
        // Can access instance variables
        System.out.println("x: " + x);
    }
}

// Usage
Example.exampleStaticMethod(); // Calls static method without creating an instance
Example obj = new Example();
obj.nonStaticMethod(); // Calls non-static method after creating an instance
```

This example demonstrates that `staticMethod` can be called without creating an instance of `Example`, while `nonStaticMethod` requires an instance (`obj`) to be called.

