In C++, static dispatch and dynamic dispatch refer to how method calls are resolved at compile time versus runtime.

1. **Static Dispatch**: This occurs when the compiler knows at compile-time which function will be called based on the type of the object at the point of the call. This is typically used in the context of virtual functions where the function to be called is determined by the static type of the pointer or reference to the object. Static dispatch is also commonly seen in non-virtual function calls where the function to be called is determined by the type of the object.

2. **Dynamic Dispatch**: This happens when the function to be called is determined at runtime based on the actual (dynamic) type of the object. Dynamic dispatch is primarily associated with virtual functions in C++, where the correct function to call is looked up in the vtable (virtual table) of the object at runtime.

Here's a simple example to illustrate:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class Base {
public:
    virtual void func() { std::cout << "Base::func()" << std::endl; }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void func() override { std::cout << "Derived::func()" << std::endl; }
};

int main() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    b->func();  // This will use dynamic dispatch and call Derived::func()
    return 0;
}
```

